--TEST--
`put`'s trailing `{ttl?: Duration}` is a lifetime the entry does not outlive: an omitted `ttl` keeps `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`'s contract, a lifetime still ahead reads back, one already over leaves nothing under the key, and the shared tier takes the same option — `rule:core-api/shape-rules`, `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's trailing options shape, and R12's unit as a
// type: the lifetime is a `Core\Time\Duration` literal, so no call site has to
// remember which scale this member chose.
var $cache = Core\Cache::memory();

// An omitted `ttl` is the contract every entry already had — present until the
// tier's cap forgets it. The option is optional here where
// `rule:core-api/a-lifetime-is-written` makes a signature's lifetime a required
// key, because nothing a cache holds leaves the process.
$cache->put("no-lifetime", "kept");

// A lifetime still ahead of every read below.
$cache->put("ahead", "kept", {ttl: 1h});

// Counted rather than read off a line: a store that answered plausibly for the
// omitted lifetime and wrongly for the written one would still print a row of
// its own.
array<string> $inside = ["no-lifetime", "ahead"];
int $present = 0;
tainted string $back = "";
foreach ($inside as string $key) {
    $back = ($cache->get($key) ?? "") as string;
    if ($back == "kept") {
        $present = $present + 1;
    }
}
echo "inside its lifetime: ", $present, " of ", Core\Arr::count($inside), "\n";

// The other side of the same bound. A lifetime that has already run out is not
// a write at all: `put` still cannot fail — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`
// gives this store no failure mode to reach for — and the key is left with
// nothing under it.
$cache->put("over", "gone", {ttl: 0s});
echo "past it: ", $cache->get("over") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// And an entry that was really there goes with it, because a `put` replaced it
// — the same exit an arrival too large for the tier takes.
$cache->put("replaced", "was here");
$cache->put("replaced", "and now not", {ttl: 0s});
echo "a lifetime already over replaces nothing: ", $cache->get("replaced") == null ? "null" : "kept", "\n";

// The shared tier takes the same option to a store that keeps its own clock, so
// the lifetime never crosses as an instant. A deployment that configured none
// is told so by name rather than handed this process's store under it.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared();
    echo "shared handed back a store\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo "the shared tier keeps its own clock, and this deployment has no store\n";
}
--EXPECT--
inside its lifetime: 2 of 2
past it: null
a lifetime already over replaces nothing: null
the shared tier keeps its own clock, and this deployment has no store
